Horizontal and Vertical Scaling

Vertical Scaling: -> scaling up
    1. We upgrade the same system rather than adding more systems. Add more power to your machine by
       adding better processors, increasing RAM, or other power-increasing adjustments.

    2. Simple to implement and useful for monolithic and small scale applications.

    Examples
        1. Upgrading a MySQL server from 16 GB RAM to 64 GB to handle more queries.
        2. Moving a website hosted on a 2-core VM to an 8-core, higher-RAM VM to improve performance.

    Disadvantages:
        1. Limited scalability: Vertical scaling is constrained by the hardware's physical limitations.
           Horizontal Scaling is not limited.
        2. One server still receives all incoming requests thus increasing the possibility of downtime in
           the event of a server failure.
        3. Scaling up often requires restarting or replacing the machine, causing downtime.

Horizontal Scaling -> scaling out
    By adding more machines or servers to distribute the workload across a larger number of individual units.
        How to distribute the workload across a larger number of individual units? [is seperate topic]
    There is no downtime while adding more servers to the network.

    Disadvantages
        1. Requires complex architecture (load balancers, distributed databases, etc.).
        2. Difficult to maintain strong consistency across distributed nodes. Requires synchronization,
           messaging, or replication between nodes.


